1
00:00:00,623 --> 00:00:04,073
(Q21（笔记填空）) HAZAL:Tom, could I ask you for some advice, please?&nbsp;

2
00:00:04,073 --> 00:00:09,123
Tom:Yes of course, if you think I can help. What's it about?&nbsp;

3
00:00:09,123 --> 00:00:17,006
HAZAL:It's my first media studies assignment, and I'm not sure how to go about it. You must have done it last year.&nbsp;

4
00:00:17,006 --> 00:00:22,796
Tom:Is that the one comparing the coverage of a particular story in a range of newspapers?&nbsp;

5
00:00:22,796 --> 00:00:23,829
HAZAL:That's right.&nbsp;

6
00:00:23,829 --> 00:00:26,626
Tom:Oh yes, I really enjoyed writing it.&nbsp;

7
00:00:26,626 --> 00:00:30,257
HAZAL:So what sort of things do I need to compare?&nbsp;

8
00:00:30,257 --> 00:00:38,117
Tom:Well, there are several things. For example, there's the question of which page of the newspaper the item appears on.&nbsp;

9
00:00:38,117 --> 00:00:45,560
Hazel:You mean, because there's a big difference between having it on the front page and the bottom of page ten, for instance?&nbsp;

10
00:00:45,560 --> 00:00:51,914
Tom:Exactly. And that shows how important the editor thinks the story is.&nbsp;

11
00:00:51,914 --> 00:01:00,474
[报道篇幅与视觉元素] Then there's the size – how many column inches the story is given, how many columns it spreads over.

12
00:01:00,474 --> 00:01:03,606
Hazel:And I suppose that includes the headline.&nbsp;

13
00:01:03,606 --> 00:01:08,074
Tom:It certainly does. It's all part of attracting the reader's attention.&nbsp;

14
00:01:08,074 --> 00:01:13,464
Hazel:What about graphics – whether there's anything visual in addition to the text?&nbsp;

15
00:01:13,464 --> 00:01:16,183
Tom:Yes, you need to consider those, too,&nbsp;

16
00:01:16,183 --> 00:01:24,071
because they can have a big effect on the reader's understanding of the story – sometimes a bigger effect than the text itself.

17
00:01:24,071 --> 00:01:30,415
[信息结构与真实性] Then you'll need to look at how the item is put together: what structure is it given?

18
00:01:30,415 --> 00:01:35,969
Bear in mind that not many people read beyond the first paragraph,

19
00:01:35,969 --> 00:01:39,138
so what has the journalist put at the beginning?

20
00:01:39,138 --> 00:01:48,257
And if, say, there are conflicting opinions about something, does one appear near the end, where people probably won't read it?

21
00:01:48,257 --> 00:01:53,985
Hazel:And newspapers sometimes give wrong or misleading information, don't they?&nbsp;

22
00:01:53,985 --> 00:01:59,042
Either deliberately or by accident. Should I be looking at that, too?

23
00:01:59,042 --> 00:02:08,951
Tom:Yes, if you can. Compare what's in different versions, and as far as possible, try and work out what's true and what isn't.&nbsp;

24
00:02:08,951 --> 00:02:15,076
[作者目的与读者预设] And that relates to a very important point: what's the writer's purpose,

25
00:02:15,076 --> 00:02:19,432
or at least the most important one, if they have several.

26
00:02:19,432 --> 00:02:26,046
It may seem to be to inform the public, but often it's that they want to create fear,

27
00:02:26,046 --> 00:02:30,443
or controversy, or to make somebody look ridiculous.

28
00:02:30,443 --> 00:02:37,477
Hazel:Gosh, I see what you mean. And I suppose the writer may make assumptions about the reader.&nbsp;

29
00:02:37,477 --> 00:02:43,600
Tom:That's right – about their knowledge of the subject, their attitudes, and their level of education,&nbsp;

30
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:49,223
which means writing so that the readers understand without feeling patronised.

31
00:02:49,223 --> 00:02:55,571
All of that will make a difference to how the story is presented.

32
00:02:55,571 --> 00:03:00,105
(Q27（Matching意愿类）) Hazel:Does it matter what type of story I write about?&nbsp;

33
00:03:00,105 --> 00:03:09,423
Tom:No – national or international politics, the arts ... Anything, as long as it's covered in two or three newspapers.&nbsp;

34
00:03:09,423 --> 00:03:15,954
Though of course it'll be easier and more fun if it's something you're interested in and know something about.

35
00:03:15,954 --> 00:03:26,117
[Q27答案句：A， 态度词 keen on] Hazel:And on that basis a national news item would be worth analysing – I'm quite keen on politics, so I'll try and find a suitable topic.&nbsp;

36
00:03:26,117 --> 00:03:29,287
(Q28) What did you choose for your analysis, Tom?&nbsp;

37
00:03:29,287 --> 00:03:34,488
Tom:I was interested in how newspapers express their opinions explicitly,&nbsp;

38
00:03:34,488 --> 00:03:45,810
so I wanted to compare editorials in different papers, but when I started looking, I couldn't find two on the same topic that I felt like analysing.

39
00:03:45,810 --> 00:03:48,619
[Q28答案句：C won't] Hazel:In that case, I won't even bother to look.&nbsp;

40
00:03:48,619 --> 00:03:55,984
(Q29) Tom:So in the end I chose a human interest story – a terribly emotional story about a young girl who was very ill,&nbsp;

41
00:03:55,984 --> 00:04:03,309
and lots of other people – mostly strangers – raised money so she could go abroad for treatment.

42
00:04:03,309 --> 00:04:09,811
Actually, I was surprised – some papers just wrote about how wonderful everyone was,

43
00:04:09,811 --> 00:04:15,901
but others considered the broader picture, like why treatment wasn't available here.

44
00:04:15,901 --> 00:04:23,184
[Q29答案句：C不会做  ，态度词：avoid] Hazel:Hmm, I usually find stories like that raise quite strong feelings in me! I'll avoid that.

45
00:04:23,184 --> 00:04:32,043
(Q30) [Q30答案句：may 可能做，态度词： think about] Perhaps I'll choose an arts topic, like different reviews of a film, or something about funding for the arts, I'll think about that.

46
00:04:32,043 --> 00:04:34,587
Tom：Yes, that might be interesting.&nbsp;

47
00:04:34,587 --> 00:04:39,068
Hazel:OK, well thanks a lot for your help, Tom. It's been really useful.&nbsp;

48
00:04:39,068 --> 00:04:41,568
Tom:You're welcome. Good luck with the assignment, Hazel.&nbsp;

